@node Expanding a key with TAB
@section Expanding a key with TAB

@cindex TAB expansion

Type @samp{<} and a key at the start of a line, then press TAB@. Leading spaces
are allowed, and the inserted text keeps the same indentation. Keys are case
sensitive, so @samp{<i} and @samp{<I} give different results. Every built-in key is a
single character. Keys that you add yourself may be longer, such as @samp{<eq}.

When the text before point is not a known key, TAB does whatever it would do
without latex-tempo, which is usually indentation.

When a template needs an argument, such as the language for @samp{<m}, the
prompt appears before the key is removed. Pressing @samp{C-g} at the prompt
leaves the buffer unchanged.

@node Choosing from a menu
@section Choosing from a menu

@cindex latex-tempo-insert-structure
@cindex C-c C-,
@cindex latex-tempo-insert-structure-by-name

@samp{C-c C-,} runs @samp{latex-tempo-insert-structure}, which mirrors
@samp{org-insert-structure-template}. It shows a menu of all templates in a small
window. Press a single key, such as @samp{u}, to insert that template. The menu
closes when you press a key or @samp{C-g}.

Press TAB or SPC in the menu to choose by name with completion instead. This
route also reaches keys longer than one character. The same completion is
available directly with @samp{M-x latex-tempo-insert-structure-by-name}.

When a region is active, environment templates such as @samp{q} wrap the whole
lines in the region, and one-line commands such as @samp{x} wrap the selected
text. Selecting the word @samp{minted} and choosing @samp{x} gives @samp{\index@{minted@}}.

@samp{M-x latex-tempo-list-templates} shows all keys in a help buffer.

